package models

import (
	"time"
)

// NotificationClaim is the record that something has already been sent.
//
// One row per thing-that-happens-once, with a unique index on the key, so the
// database decides who got there first. That is the whole mechanism, and the
// reason it is a table rather than a cache entry: a Redis key that expires or
// is lost on a restart turns "remind this customer once" into "remind this
// customer again", and the customer is the one who finds out.
//
// # Why a claim and not a log
//
// The row is written BEFORE the notification is sent, and removed if the send
// fails. So the question it answers is "has anybody started sending this",
// which is the question that matters when two replicas run the same sweep a
// second apart. A row written after a successful send answers a different
// question, and leaves a window in which both replicas think nobody has sent
// it yet.
//
// The cost of claiming first is that a process which dies between claiming and
// sending loses that one notification. That is the deliberate trade: losing one
// overdue reminder is a smaller harm than sending it every thirty seconds until
// somebody notices, which is what the alternative does when a sweep retries.
//
// # It grows, and it is not swept
//
// Nothing deletes these rows, because deleting a claim makes its notification
// sendable again, and a retention sweep would quietly re-send everything older
// than the window. One row is around a hundred bytes: a reminder per invoice
// per month on ten thousand invoices is twelve megabytes a year. If a project
// ever does need to reclaim that space, the keys are human-readable and the
// safe thing to delete is the ones whose subject no longer exists.
type NotificationClaim struct {
	// Key is what was claimed, as the caller spelled it, with the scope that
	// was active prefixed. 191 characters because MySQL cannot index a longer
	// utf8mb4 column, and a key longer than that is hashed by the caller of
	// notify.Once rather than silently truncated here.
	Key       string    `gorm:"primarykey;size:191" json:"key"`
	CreatedAt time.Time `gorm:"index" json:"created_at"`
}

// TableName is given rather than derived, because the derived name for this
// one is notification_claims either way and saying so costs nothing next to
// finding out that it was not.
func (NotificationClaim) TableName() string { return "notification_claims" }
